                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_conscious:     `<strong>Anchoring on conscious premium drinkers is the right segment.</strong> These are the shoppers who already pay for quality and will pay more for a credible sustainability story — exactly where your true 100%-certified asset converts to a premium.`,
                    coach_r1_mass:          `<strong>"Everyone who buys coffee" is not a position.</strong> Go broad and you compete on price and reach with a multinational and a private label — the two places you lose. A brand built on meaning needs a segment that values that meaning.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>Chasing the trade-down shopper drags you into a price fight you can't win.</strong> The private label is structurally cheaper; meeting it on price erodes the very premium your sustainability story should defend.`,
                    coach_r1_rivalfans:     `<strong>Out-spending the multinational for its own audience is a losing budget war.</strong> You have ₡480M; they have a "Planet Positive" machine. Win on the ground you own — a sustainability story that is actually true — not on theirs.`,
                    coach_r1_core:          `<strong>That is a real position.</strong> Making sustainability the meaning — the only coffee sourced 100% from certified cooperatives at a verified farmer premium — is what the rival cannot truthfully say. Now everything downstream can reinforce it.`,
                    coach_r1_taste:         `<strong>"Great taste, sustainability in the footer" is everyone's position.</strong> Conejo's whole point is that sustainability has to be the meaning of the brand, not a sticker. If you removed the sustainability line, would your position change at all? If not, you don't have one.`,
                    coach_r1_posprice:      `<strong>Positioning on value invites the private-label comparison you most want to avoid.</strong> You will be measured on price, where you are structurally beaten, and the genuine sustainability asset goes unused.`,
                    coach_r1_weakproof:     `<strong>A position built on thin proof points is a claim waiting to be challenged.</strong> Your asset is real — 100% certified, a verified farmer premium. Anchor the position on those specifics, not on vague intentions, or the perception won't move.`,
                    coach_r2_specific:      `<strong>Specific, verified claims are the durable play.</strong> "100% sourced from certified Tarrazú cooperatives, farmer premium paid" is a claim the rival can't make and a journalist can't break. It builds trust more slowly than a bold slogan — and it lasts.`,
                    coach_r2_broad:         `<strong>Bold, broad "eco" language moves perception fast — and is a loaded gun.</strong> "The planet's most sustainable coffee" sounds great until someone asks which standard you mean. You have a real story; make sure your proof can carry the claim before you make it.`,
                    coach_r2_timid:         `<strong>You played it so safe your campaign never says you source 100% from certified cooperatives.</strong> You are sitting on the category's best sustainability story and you whispered it. Trust requires verifiable claims — but it also requires that you make them. What are you afraid of?`,
                    coach_r2_generic:       `<strong>Sustainability as a small line nobody can challenge is sustainability nobody will notice.</strong> The whole asset — 100% certified sourcing — is unsaid. You have protected yourself from a complaint by guaranteeing the perception score barely moves.`,
                    coach_r2_greenwash:     `<strong>Your campaign claims more than you can prove — a consumer advocacy group just filed a complaint and a journalist is asking which standard you mean.</strong> You had a 100%-certified, farmer-premium true story and you overclaimed past it into greenwashing. What did vagueness buy you that your real proof points wouldn't?`,
                    coach_r2_underclaim:    `<strong>You moved perception but barely claimed the truth.</strong> The genuine 100%-certified story is your sharpest weapon — pushing the claim too gently leaves most of that asset on the table.`,
                    coach_r2_substantiated: `<strong>That is substance over signal.</strong> A specific, verified claim carried by farmer traceability, certification and impact reporting — and not pushed past the evidence — is exactly Conejo's principle: a brand converting genuine sustainability into trusted meaning.`,
                    coach_r3_tvheavy:       `<strong>You put most of ₡480M into TV and streaming. Awareness will climb — and you just blew through your conversion and credibility budgets.</strong> Your contribution margin is sliding toward the floor and the board sees a brand that's famous and unprofitable. Reach without conversion is vanity. Where does the equity actually convert to sales?`,
                    coach_r3_copilot:       `<strong>Using AI as a co-pilot is the disciplined choice.</strong> Let it sharpen segments and the media mix in minutes, then validate every recommendation against your actual buyers. That is AI-enabled marketing with human judgment in the loop.`,
                    coach_r3_ignoreai:      `<strong>Refusing the AI tool entirely leaves value on the table.</strong> You hand-built segments the tool would have sharpened in minutes. AI is a co-pilot worth using — the judgment stays yours, but the leverage is real.`,
                    coach_r3_autopilot:     `<strong>You let the AI set your entire media mix and never checked it — it over-indexed on a segment that's a sliver of your buyers.</strong> AI is a co-pilot, not the captain. Where is your judgment in the loop?`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>Honest KPI accounting is what the board can act on.</strong> Reporting what ₡480M actually moved, naming the slowest KPI, and showing the margin held the floor reads as less impressive today and far more credible when they check it.`,
                    coach_r4_inflated:      `<strong>You're claiming awareness up 20, equity up 18, top sustainability perception AND positive ROI in four quarters on ₡480M.</strong> The reach math doesn't support all four. Which number is aspirational, and what would you cut to make the rest real?`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>Leading with awareness alone is the vanity metric.</strong> Awareness is the easiest number to buy and the weakest proof that the brand was built. The board wants equity, perception and margin — not just how many people have heard of you.`,
                    coach_r4_premium:       `<strong>Defending the premium on brand equity is the whole point.</strong> A substantiated sustainability meaning is what stops the trade-down to private label — that is brand as margin defense, and it protects the premium you spent four quarters rebuilding.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>Matching private label on price surrenders the premium you just rebuilt.</strong> You compress margin toward the floor and tell the market your brand isn't worth more — undoing the equity work in one decision.`,
                    coach_r4_coherent:      `<strong>That is one coherent system.</strong> The position, the verified campaign, the equity-building media mix and an honest scorecard all tell the same story — sustainability converted into trusted meaning and a defensible premium. That is the brand the board mandated.`,
                    coach_r4_incoherent:    `<strong>A plan defended as three loose pieces doesn't hold.</strong> If the positioning, campaign and media aren't one system, the board sees the seams — and so does the journalist. Equity is built by coherence, not by three good slides.`,

