                    insight_trusted:        `Trusted sustainable brand: you converted a genuine 100%-certified asset into real equity and perception, kept awareness climbing, and held the 18% margin floor. The premium is defended on meaning, and the rival's thinner "Planet Positive" story now reads hollow next to yours.`,
                    insight_greenwash:      `Greenwashing backlash: you claimed more than you could prove and handed the journalist the story. A consumer-advocacy complaint and a credibility collapse cost you the very trust the campaign was meant to build — the bold claim was the most expensive mistake.`,
                    insight_wasted:         `Asset wasted: you sat on the category's best sustainability story — 100% certified sourcing, a verified farmer premium — and whispered it. Perception barely moved because timidity, not greenwashing, was your error. Trust requires that you actually make the claim.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully integrate the four decisions. The position, the campaign, the media mix and the scorecard tell slightly different stories — enough for the board to see the seams and the premium to keep eroding.`,
                    insight_margin:         `Margin floor breached: contribution margin fell below 18%, the board's hard constraint. Famous and unprofitable is the worst outcome — likely too much mass-reach TV or trade spend with too little conversion. Reach without margin is the road to a downmarket reposition.`,
                    insight_margin_strong:  `Margin discipline held: you rebuilt the brand while keeping contribution margin comfortably above the 18% floor. Equity built on a respected margin is the kind the board actually rewards.`,
                    insight_roi_negative:   `Campaign ROI came in negative: too much spend on vanity reach and too little where equity converts to sales (trade, D2C, targeted digital). The mix bought fame, not return.`,
                    insight_roi_strong:     `Strong campaign ROI: the mix put money where equity converts — targeted digital, trade and D2C — so the spend returned rather than just raising awareness.`,
                    insight_converted:      `Sustainability converted: perception and equity both crossed into healthy territory, which means the genuine asset is now trusted meaning rather than an unseen fact. That is the disclosure-performance gap closed — and what defends the price premium.`,
                    insight_vanity:         `Awareness ran ahead of equity — the classic vanity gap. People have heard of you, but the brand isn't yet worth more to them. Awareness is the cheapest KPI to buy and the weakest proof that a brand was built.`,

                    hint_margin:            `Contribution margin has breached the 18% floor — the board's hard constraint. Pull back trade spend and mass-reach TV, and shift to channels where equity converts without compressing margin.`,
                    hint_greenwash:         `Your campaign overclaimed past the evidence and triggered a greenwashing complaint. Pull the claim back to your verifiable, specific story — 100% certified sourcing — and let the proof mechanism carry it.`,
                    hint_wasted:            `You're whispering the category's best sustainability story. Claim the true 100%-certified, farmer-premium specifics — timidity wastes the asset just as surely as overclaiming destroys it.`,
                    hint_vanity:            `Awareness is running ahead of equity — vanity reach. Shift spend to conversion and credibility channels (digital storytelling, influencer/PR, D2C) so the fame turns into a brand worth a premium.`,
                    hint_sustain:           `Sustainability perception is still low with little time left — the very score your true asset should dominate. Substantiate a specific claim and weight the mix toward credibility, or the board sees an unconverted asset.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — equity, sustainability perception and a margin above the floor all healthy. Lock in an honest scorecard and defend the premium on equity to hold it through the board review.`,

